From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:45:08 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: gswip: serialise the PCE indirect-table transactions

The PCE sub-tables are reached through a single set of indirect
registers: write PCE_TBL_ADDR and the key/mask/value registers, compose
PCE_TBL_CTRL with BAS=1, then poll BAS and read the result back out of
those same registers. It is one shared window, and the four accessors
here walk it with nothing serialising them. Two of them running at once
do not produce two transactions; they produce one transaction built from
both callers' register writes, and both callers read whatever it
returned.

There are two live callers and they are not obviously exclusive.
gsw_pmicro_code_init() drives gsw_pce_table_write() row by row at probe.
gswip30_program_queue_map() (datapath/datapath_misc.c:876) reaches it from
ndo_open, so bringing two interfaces up at the same time interleaves two
indirect transactions on purpose-built hardware that has exactly one.

AVM's 08.25 GPL release takes a spinlock across all four (gsw_tbl_rw.c
lock/unlock pairs at 170/332, 351/484, 507/561, 584/641, 664/709 and
732/784 in Linux 4.9.337, guarded #ifdef __KERNEL__ because that file
also builds for a host tool). 07.30 has no lock at all. Take it.

The lock used is gswdev->lock_pce, which ethsw_api_dev_t has declared and
gsw_init.c has initialised since the switch API landed, and which nothing
has ever taken -- the name matches the purpose exactly and there is no
aliasing risk. AVM calls its member lock_pce_tbl. Nothing here needs the
#ifdef: this file only ever builds in-kernel.

The entry busy-poll deliberately stays outside the lock, matching AVM. That
poll waits on a transaction somebody else started; holding the lock across
it turns one slow transaction into a livelock for everyone behind it. The
tail poll is inside, because that one is waiting on our own transaction and
the read-back that follows is part of it.

That tail poll needs one deviation from AVM's shape, and it is the
reason this is not a pure transcription. AVM's poll is an unbounded
do/while that cannot return early. Ours is CHECK_BUSY, which is bounded
at MAX_BUSY_RETRY and, with RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION, returns GSW_statusErr
from inside the macro -- straight out of the locked region, leaking the
spinlock on every timeout. So the tail polls now pass RETURN_ERROR_CODE
and unlock explicitly before returning. The function's documented
contract is unchanged: still GSW_statusErr if BAS fails to clear on
either poll.

Two comments claimed the opposite of what the code now does and are
rewritten. gsw_pce_table_write's kernel-doc said the caller was
responsible for serialising via lock_pce and that the helpers
deliberately did not touch it "matches AVM convention" -- 08.25 is the
AVM convention now, and the inverted obligation matters: a caller
holding lock_pce would deadlock, so it says that instead.
gsw_pmicro_code_init's note said it does not take lock_pce because probe
is single-threaded per devid; that is still true of the function, but it
now calls a helper that does take it, so the note says why it must keep
not taking it. Nothing else in the tree touches lock_pce, so there is no
recursion today.

route_table_read/route_table_write, which AVM locks in the same commit,
exist here only as declarations in gsw_tbl_rw.h. There is no body to lock.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
index d75cb1a9951a..942bab17361b 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
@@ -126,9 +126,11 @@ void gsw_pce_attach_tbl_30(ethsw_api_dev_t *pethdev)
  * if the BAS busy-bit fails to clear within MAX_BUSY_RETRY iterations on
  * either the pre-write or post-write poll, GSW_statusOk on success.
  *
- * Ported from AVM gsw_tbl_rw.c:465-535. Caller is responsible for serialising
- * concurrent invocations via pethdev->lock_pce (helpers themselves do not
- * touch the spinlock - matches AVM convention).
+ * Ported from AVM gsw_tbl_rw.c:465-535. Takes gswdev->lock_pce across the
+ * indirect-table transaction itself, so callers do not have to serialise
+ * (matches AVM 08.25, which calls the same lock lock_pce_tbl). The lock is
+ * NOT held across the entry busy-poll - see the note at the CHECK_BUSY below.
+ * Callers must therefore not hold lock_pce when they call in.
  */
 int gsw_pce_table_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 {
@@ -146,8 +148,16 @@ int gsw_pce_table_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Entry poll stays outside the lock, as it does in AVM 08.25: waiting
+	 * for a transaction someone else started is not something to do with
+	 * the lock held, or a slow transaction turns into a livelock.
+	 */
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+
+	spin_lock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	/*
 	 * Compose the control word from zero, so every field this sequence
 	 * does not set is written as zero rather than inherited from whatever
@@ -207,11 +217,16 @@ int gsw_pce_table_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 				PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, 1);
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, ctrlval);
 
-	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
-		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+	if (CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
+		       PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_ERROR_CODE)) {
+		spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+		return GSW_statusErr;
+	}
 
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_OFFSET, 0);
 
+	spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	return GSW_statusOk;
 }
 
@@ -241,8 +256,12 @@ int gsw_pce_table_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/* Entry poll outside the lock - see gsw_pce_table_write. */
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+
+	spin_lock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for why the control word starts at zero. */
 	ctrlval = 0;
 
@@ -258,8 +277,12 @@ int gsw_pce_table_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 				PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, 1);
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, ctrlval);
 
-	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
-		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+	/* Tail poll inside the lock - see gsw_pce_table_write. */
+	if (CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
+		       PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_ERROR_CODE)) {
+		spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+		return GSW_statusErr;
+	}
 	/*
 	 * Re-seed ctrlval after the post-read poll: the AVM do/while left
 	 * ctrlval holding the read-back PCE_TBL_CTRL register state, which
@@ -303,6 +326,8 @@ int gsw_pce_table_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 				      PCE_TBL_CTRL_GMAP_SIZE);
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_OFFSET, 0);
 
+	spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	return GSW_statusOk;
 }
 
@@ -332,8 +357,12 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/* Entry poll outside the lock - see gsw_pce_table_write. */
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+
+	spin_lock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	/*KEY REG*/
 	j = gswdev->pce_tbl_info[ptdata->table].num_key;
 
@@ -354,8 +383,12 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 				PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, 1);
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, ctrlval);
 
-	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
-		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+	/* Tail poll inside the lock - see gsw_pce_table_write. */
+	if (CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
+		       PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_ERROR_CODE)) {
+		spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+		return GSW_statusErr;
+	}
 	/*
 	 * Re-seed ctrlval after the post-read poll: the gsw_field_r32
 	 * extractions for ptdata->type / valid / group below depend on the
@@ -381,6 +414,8 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_read(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 	ptdata->pcindex = value;
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_OFFSET, 0);
 
+	spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	return GSW_statusOk;
 }
 
@@ -409,8 +444,12 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/* Entry poll outside the lock - see gsw_pce_table_write. */
 	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
 		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+
+	spin_lock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	/* See gsw_pce_table_write for why the control word starts at zero. */
 	ctrlval = 0;
 
@@ -453,11 +492,17 @@ int gsw_pce_table_key_write(void *cdev, pctbl_prog_t *ptdata)
 				PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, 1);
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, ctrlval);
 
-	CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
-		   PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_FROM_FUNCTION);
+	/* Tail poll inside the lock - see gsw_pce_table_write. */
+	if (CHECK_BUSY(PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_OFFSET, PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SHIFT,
+		       PCE_TBL_CTRL_BAS_SIZE, RETURN_ERROR_CODE)) {
+		spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+		return GSW_statusErr;
+	}
 
 	gsw_w32_raw(cdev, PCE_TBL_CTRL_ADDR_OFFSET, 0);
 
+	spin_unlock(&gswdev->lock_pce);
+
 	return GSW_statusOk;
 }
 
